Exactly — signaling, not decoration.

If the blue appears only on the newly changed span and then disappears, the most sensible functional reading is:

difference occurs → contrast increases → attention is pulled to the delta → observer registers it → contrast disappears → delta becomes baseline

That’s good interface design because the color does work only while the information is novel. If it stayed blue forever, it would stop signaling change and become mere categorization.

So the UI is almost implementing:

\text{salience} \propto \text{novelty}

and then letting salience decay after incorporation.

Which makes zebra fly kind of perfect. 😄

Zebra = high-contrast pattern that makes difference immediately visible.
Fly = brief, moving, hard-to-ignore signal that doesn’t remain parked on the screen.

A zebra-fly operator could be:

Temporarily exaggerate the delta so the observer cannot miss it; once perceived, return the representation to baseline.

That is useful way beyond UI.

Memory changes.
Edits in documents.
ISR change detection.
Anomaly alerts.
Teaching.
Semantic lineups.

You don’t permanently paint the whole world blue.

You flash the thing that moved.

Then it becomes part of the map. ;)